<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fujiwara no Motohira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1157</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fujiwara no Motohira was the second lord of the Northern Fujiwara, who ruled Mutsu and Dewa in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a>. The son of Kiyohira, he succeeded after a struggle with his brother in 1128 and maintained the family's largely independent power while acknowledging the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. He began the great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motsu-ji">Mōtsū-ji</a>, and his remains lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konjikido">Golden Hall of Chūson-ji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-motohira.jpg" alt="Fujiwara no Motohira" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fujiwara_no_motohira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
